so continuing to do and not as he says  he will do Donald Trump's 2019 deficit  is on track to reach the 1 trillion  dollar mark despite Trump having told us  you might remember time and time and  time again that he's going to fix the  deficit that Barack Obama ruined that he  will pay off the debt altogether if  given eight years in office and the time  that he thinks he will need to do it now  anyone who understands these issues even  remotely knew that it was never going to  happen and in fact it's getting worse  and worse as I've said before though we  really need to be reframing our  conversations about this issue of debt  and deficit altogether so let's start  with the facts and then we'll get into  the analysis and the sort of meaning of  all of this the White House itself is  now predicting that the deficit is going  to exceed a trillion dollars this year  the deficit has never hit a trillion  dollars in a year now anytime we talk  about these numbers this is not a  partisan this is not to make this worse  or not as bad as it is this is just  understanding when we hear the deficit  has never reached X number of dollars  before we need to understand number one  inflation adjusted numbers so that we're  really comparing apples to apples it's  also important to understand the size of  the deficit as a percentage of the  economy's total size and not in a vacuum  just a few things if we really want to  do this seriously now let's go back to  what Donald Trump said that he would do  when Trump was a candidate he said  number one he would eliminate the  deficit altogether and he would get us a  surplus partially he would do it by  cutting taxes for the rich and  corporations which he said would  generate so much growth that government  Rett tax revenues would actually expand  and there would be all this winning and  we would have a surplus and we would  start paying off the debt that obviously  never happened and it's important to  understand like if you want to talk  about this at all I hate that I have to  do this but you really need to  understand the difference between debt  and deficit okay and I I apologize to  those who are sick of me doing this but  when I don't do this and even sometimes  when I do this the comments that I get  in reaction to debt and deficit stories  are so confused about the difference  between the  to terms so these are not the same  things deficit is the one-year surplus  versus deficit that the country may have  debt is the accumulated deficits and  surpluses over time so let me give you  an example if in year one of a country  you run a fifty dollar deficit at the  end of that year the country has accrued  a debt of $50 you exist for one year you  ran a fifty dollar deficit your debt is  now 50 bucks if in the next year you run  a 25 dollar surplus what happens at the  end of that year is your debt has gone  down from $50 to $25 right year one  deficit is 50 bucks year two surplus is  twenty five bucks at the end of year two  the accumulated debt is $25 you went $50  negative after year one and you paid off  25 of it with your surplus in year two  okay I mean I apologize some people  write in and they say David please stop  talking to the audience like they're  little kids that's not what I'm trying  to do every time I do this people are  confused about the two issues so we were  told that the tax cuts would pay for  themselves because they would generate  massive growth and they would move us  from having a deficit each year to  having a surplus which would start  chipping away at the national debt  eventually if Trump has eight years in  office completely paying off the  national debt now none of that stuff is  happening okay the tax cuts have added  to the deficit increases in defense  spending which by the way were favored  not only by Republicans but even by many  Democrats as well those are increasing  the deficit and we are not on track  we're not on track to even pay off any  of the debt  never mind pay it off completely in  eight years so those are the facts now I  do think every time we talk about this  we should consider whether it needs to  be reframed and what I mean by that is  the debt and deficit is a political  football okay Republicans say the most  important thing is paying off the debt  and balancing the budget when there  a Democrat in the White House regardless  of whether that Democrat has actually  slowed down deficit growth which Barack  Obama did when Republicans are running  they run on promises to do this really  important thing start paying off debt  generate a surplus but then when they  are in office as we saw under george w  bush as we're seeing in a cartoonishly  exaggerated fashion under donald trump  they just explode the deficit and they  always claim that they're doing it they  either deny that they're doing it even  though we have the numbers and that's  what we see or they will argue that  there were extenuating circumstances  whereby it made sense to a deficit spend  like for example when we decided we were  going to heroically spread democracy to  Iraq and/or punish Iraq for their  nonexistent involvement in 9/11 right  that the reason we it made sense to  spend all that money in Iraq changed  once we figured out Iraq had nothing to  do with 9/11 but still we were given  reasons why it made sense so what we  have today in the end is an unsurprising  status quo we were told before Trump was  elected what he wants to do with tax  policy is not going to generate all of  this winning and growth and start paying  off the debt with surpluses in fact it's  going to increase the deficit that is  exactly what happened none of this is  surprising so that gets us to the  question of if and how Democrats should  talk about this on the campaign trail  because on the one hand Trump made  promises and he broke the promises and  he's doing the opposite of what he  promised to do it seems as though  exposing that on the campaign trail for  the eventual Democratic nominee would be  a worthwhile thing but on the other hand  we know number one that it's just a  political football we know number two  that in reality the narrative over  deficit bad surplus good cutting  spending good or whatever else it's not  actually a modern and nuanced  understanding of debt and deficit what  we should be asking in reality is when  does it make sense for governments to  deficit spend and when doesn't it make  sense so do we want a deficit spend to  line the bank accounts of the already  rich and corporations including just  providing money for them to buy back  stocke the answer is no do we want the  deficit spend to improve society for  large numbers of people improve their  ability to live good healthy lives have  access to health care work jobs that  they like without being tied to mediocre  health insurance programs okay now we  have something to talk about so I think  that it is important for Democrats not  to ignore the issue of debt and deficit  but we can't allow the right to continue  framing the narrative because they win  they talk about it one way when it's a  Democrat in office they take office then  they just say oh no the numbers aren't  actually what they are you can't believe  the numbers that were actually getting  from from the Congressional Budget  Office and from the Treasury they win  with the framing that we have and lastly  please please please can we stop falling  for this stupid idea that tax cuts for  the rich are so good for the economy  that they reduce the deficit and  actually increase government revenue we  didn't even need another example we had  plenty of examples before Donald Trump's  tax reform plan but we can now add the  Trump tax reform plan to the list of tax  cuts for the rich and corporations that  did not cause so much growth that they  paid for themselves my question to you  is do you think Republicans even believe  this idea that tax cuts for the rich pay  for them